stria  that and the fact that the wizarding  authorities thought it would be  entertaining to imprison him inside the  walls that he built  if nurman guard didn't exist then i'm  almost certain that he would have ended  up in azkaban  years later it was reported that  nurmengard had deteriorated  significantly  it no longer had the upkeep of  grindelwald's acolytes and it simply  served as a prison which it grew to look  like more and more as the years passed  eventually nurmengard was nothing more  than a giant towering building made of  black rock that can only be described as  grim and ominous  2. azkaban azkaban was built in the 15th  century and began being used as a  detention facility in the year 1718  housing some of the most sinister  witches and wizards to have ever  disgraced the wizarding world  the name azkaban is a mixture of the  word alcatraz which refers to a famous  mughal island prison and abaddon which  is a hebrew word that means place of  destruction  sounds wonderful doesn't it  at one point in time before the fortress  was built azkaban was just an island but  when its first inhabitant ecrisdis found  the island he decided to transform it  into his own little dark arts experiment  ecrisdis was a dark wizard that  practiced the worst forms of dark magic  and he was known to lure sailors to the  island then torture them  before he got there he was crazy but  after spending a considerable amount of  time there he went completely bonkers  while the christus lived there he placed  concealment charms on the island so that  no one would be able to find it and  after he died these concealment charms  were finally lifted  this is when the british ministry of  magic first discovered the island the  investigative team from the ministry who  first went to azkaban refused to discuss  their findings other than the fact that  the island was infested with dementors  over the course of its history azkaban  has been home to many witches and  wizards including percival dumbledore  martinez crouch jr various lestrangers  and various gaunts just to name a few  though it was under much scrutiny  initially when the ministry recognized  that they could utilize the island to  imprison evildoers they capitalized on  this and began sentencing people to  azgaban  the ministry of magic felt that the  smaller existing wizarding prisons that  were scattered across the country  imposed a considerable security risk and  put citizens in danger  azkaban serves as the ultimate prison  due to its remote location and the sea  of dementors that make sure the  prisoners are in check  escaping from azkaban before your  sentence is over is punishable by fate  worse than death a dementor's kiss  the island itself is located in the  north sea  but the island is said to have never  appeared on any map muggle or magical  it's suspected that the island was  either created or enlarged via magical  means i'm thinking that accrisdas was  probably responsible for that  the north sea is a marginal sea of the  atlantic ocean located between great  britain denmark norway germany the  netherlands belgium and france  if you're going to get locked up try  your absolute best to avoid azkaban  3. irkstarg  first witnessed by fans in the new  secrets of dumbledore film the irkstark  was a wizarding prison located in berlin  germany that was used by the german  ministry of magic  the irkstarg could be accessed via an  underground tunnel system and many  believe that the prison had been  publicly abandoned  however as it turns out and as we  witnessed in these secrets of dumbledore  the german ministry were still secretly  holding people at the facility by 1932  at the entrance to the prison there was  a prison guard a warder who would  confiscate each prisoner's personal  belongings upon their arrival  the prison itself which had a circular  shape and spiraled downwards was dark  scary and certainly not the sort of  place you'd want to end up particularly  as your chances of getting out at least  from the way the film portrayed it were  likely quite slim  from what appeared to be near the bottom  of the endless spiraling prison  structure was a giant manticore that  would devour prisoners as soon as their  lanterns went out  dumbledore once referred to irkstarg as  the german ministry's bed and breakfast  4. macoosa prison macusa aka the magical  congress of the united states of america  was essentially the american ministry of  magic and just like the german ministry  of magic macusa 2 had their own prison  directly linked with their organization  the macusa headquarters was located in  the woolworth building in downtown new  york city and the prison cells were  located in the basement  it's entirely unclear how long these  prison cells have existed but provided  that macusa was founded in 1693  i'd wager it's been a long time  yes the woolworth building was completed  in 1913 but provided the prison cells  were located in the basement of the  building it's entirely possible that  they were there before the building had  even been built hiding underground  reportedly the main cell room held  hundreds of cage-like cells stacked on  top of each other  sort of like what you'd expect to see at  a dog pound and in the middle of the  main room there was a large staircase  with iron bars on both sides that led up  to the rooftop of the woolworth building  though the building was clean and  relatively well kept it certainly didn't  mean that prisoners there had a good  experience  in fact prisoners were kept in strangely  dark rooms devoid of windows accessible  only through one ominous looking iron  door with a tiny sliding window big  enough only to fit your eyes  5. dark tower hogwarts a prison at  hogwarts yes you heard me right  just near the transfiguration courtyard  at hogwarts you may recall a tall narrow  tower  this dark looking tower aptly named the  dark tower was in fact used as a prison  at hogwarts however provided that this  prison was at a school for children  and given that there were only a few  cells i'd probably call it more of a  holding facility the tower itself was  seven stories tall  and sported four gargoyles on each  corner of the tower directing water away  from it when it rained it has also been  reported that the prison cells were not  outfitted with any kind of incantations  making it a lot less secure than other  wizarding prisons we know  while in the present day there may not  be much need for a prison cell at  hogwarts we do have to remember that  back in the day before azgaban prisons  were scattered all across the country  having a holding cell or at least  something at hogwarts a massive hub for  witches and wizards probably made a lot  of sense  in the film version of the prisoner of  azkaban sirius black is held prisoner in  this tower  however in the books he's kept in  phillies flipwick's office over in the  west tower  6. new york witch's penitentiary  the new york witch's penitentiary first  mentioned on a poster in fantastic  beasts and where to find them was a  women's only wizarding prison located in  the state of new york  past prisoners include madame miraforum  eleanor shanks and matilda's heisenberg  all of these women were highly dangerous  criminals at large with crimes ranging  from no maj murder to atrocities against  the wizarding community  i feel like bellatrix would have fit  right in here  and that pretty much concludes the list  of the main prisons the only other two  wizarding prisons if you could call them  that that i know of are the abandoned  substation which was used as a holding  facility for snatchers to hold  muggleborns and the abandoned nuclear  power plant which was used to imprison  enemies of the death eater controlled  ministry of magic  these facilities appeared in the harry  potter games  and can be seen in the background of  some of the harry potter film  promotional posters  and that's it for this video did i miss  any had you heard of all of these  prisons leave a comment down below  if you enjoyed the content please like  the video and subscribe to the channel  until next time remember it does not do  to dwell on dreams and forget to live  [Music]  you

